title The source that vanished mid-compile
prompt Keaton -- capture the failure
think Six earlier attempts came back green because most were SINGLE witnesses, and the reds live in the batch. Running the whole set eight at a time and stopping on the first failure answered 13 in one round.
obs THE CAUSE IS THE BUILD'S INPUT RATHER THAN ITS OUTPUT. Ten of the thirteen say it in their own words: unable to load pan.zig, clock.zig, timeline.zig, envelope.zig, transport.zig -- a SOURCE file missing mid-compile, never a binary.
obs THE TOOL'S OWN SOURCE NAMES THE MECHANISM. rye/src/main.rye bridges each .rye to an adjacent .zig in the SOURCE directory and, at line 371, writes ephemeral .zig files the toolchain can read, then clears them all away. Two concurrent builds over one module generate the same shadow names and one deletes them while the other is still compiling.
obs CONFIRMED THREE WAYS rather than argued: every named file is a SHARED import -- timeline.rye by 211 lotus modules, pan.rye by 113, clock.rye by 41 -- the failure counts track that popularity, and lotus/*.zig reads zero at rest, which is what ephemeral means.
obs SO THE PENS COULD NEVER HAVE REACHED IT, and the earlier lap was right to say so without knowing why. A pen holds the OUTPUT; the shadows are the INPUT, in a namespace every module in the room shares.
obs THE THREE REMAINING FAILURES SAY ONLY Lattice build failed, because their assert discards the build error. That is the mute shape %700's own header warns of, and it is why a fortnight of these left no evidence a hand could read.
obs NAMED THREE DOORS AND TOOK NONE. A per-module build lock, shadows emitted into a per-build directory, or content-addressed shadow names -- which the bridge's own import rewriting makes thinkable. Each changes how the compiler bridge works, so each wants Keaton's word rather than my lap.
loom capture round=1 parallelism=8 witnesses=279 failures=13 named_reason=10 mute=3
loom imports timeline=211 pan=113 clock=41 envelope=23 transport=7 zig_at_rest=0
rota none -- a capture run on Keaton's word, no rota this lap
